Automated extraction of biotic interactions from scientific literature
enables large-scale knowledge graph construction over biodiversity corpora,
where systematic manual curation is infeasible given the scale of the
available literature. We present a multi-task transformer system combining
three components: a two-stage teacher chain in which a large language
model annotates harvested sentences with binary interaction labels and a
trained classifier ensemble subsequently generates calibrated soft
probability labels for knowledge distillation; a multi-task BiomedBERT
architecture that jointly classifies sentences and annotates biological
entities using automatically derived sequence labels; and a warm-start
initialization strategy that preserves fine-tuned encoder representations
while training the entity recognition head jointly from the first epoch.
On a 500-sentence multi-source test set, the system achieves F1\,=\,0.874,
of which a +0.162\,F1 gain comes from soft-label distillation alone. This
gain is shown to be primarily a calibration effect rather than a ranking
improvement: the same comparison moves the area under the ROC curve by
only +0.024, indicating soft labels chiefly correct the decision threshold
rather than the model's underlying discriminative power. The system
matches a strong single-task baseline while additionally producing
structured entity output at no extra inference cost, and on
real-literature sources alone it outperforms that baseline, with the
aggregate tie driven entirely by a synthetic test subset. The classifier
is deployed upstream of a semantic search platform for biotic
interactions, demonstrating a practical path from unstructured text to
structured, searchable biodiversity knowledge.
